==English==

===Etymology===
From West Indian {{bor|en|es|dengue}} in 1828, from the [[Kiswahili]] term ''{{lang|sw|dinga}}'' (in full ''kidingapopo'' or ''kidinga pepo'', "a kind of sudden cramp-like seizure caused by an evil spirit", ''dinga'' itself meaning sudden cramp-like seizure). The borrowed term changed to ''dengue'' in Spanish due to this word existing in Spanish with the meaning "fastidiousness" and the [[folk etymology]] referring to the dislike of movement by affected patients of [[chikungunya]] which was also reffered to as dengue until the first half of the 20th century.<ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20220717162940/https://www.lexico.com/definition/dengue dengue] in Oxford Dictionaries</ref><ref>Scott B. Halstead, [https://wwwnc.cdc.gov/eid/article/21/4/14-1723_article Reappearance of Chikungunya, Formerly Called Dengue, in the Americas], Emerging Infectious Diseases, Volume 21, Number 4—April 2015</ref>

